# GameVoice

**Reads a game's on-screen dialogue aloud, giving each character their own voice.**

[![tests](https://github.com/TheMrMackay/gamevoice/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/TheMrMackay/gamevoice/actions/workflows/ci.yml)
[![licence](https://img.shields.io/badge/licence-MIT-blue.svg)](LICENSE)
[![python](https://img.shields.io/badge/python-3.10%2B-blue.svg)](https://www.python.org/downloads/)
[![platform](https://img.shields.io/badge/platform-Windows%2010%20%7C%2011-lightgrey.svg)](#requirements)

</div>

---

Works with **any** game, because it reads the screen rather than the game. No
mods, no memory reading, no per-game plugin. A game nobody has ever configured
still gets read on first launch.

- **Entirely offline.** Nothing is uploaded. No account, no API key.
- **841 voices**, tagged male or female by measurement, assigned per character.
- **CPU only.** Synthesis never touches the GPU, so it costs you no frames.

## Install

```powershell
git clone https://github.com/TheMrMackay/gamevoice.git
cd gamevoice
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File install.ps1
```

The installer finds a Python 3.10+, builds an isolated environment inside the
folder, fetches the voices, and makes Desktop and Start Menu shortcuts. It
writes nothing to the registry and modifies no system Python.

| Flag | Effect |
|---|---|
| `-NoVoices` | Skip the ~260 MB download and use the Windows built-in voices |
| `-NoShortcuts` | Do not create Desktop or Start Menu entries |
| `-Python <path>` | Use a specific `python.exe` |

### Uninstall

```powershell
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File uninstall.ps1
```

Stops GameVoice if it is running, then removes the environment, the voice models
and the shortcuts. **Your tuned profiles and settings are kept** — they are work
a download cannot replace. Then delete the folder.

| Flag | Effect |
|---|---|
| `-WhatIf` | Show what would go, remove nothing |
| `-RemoveSettings` | Also delete `%LOCALAPPDATA%\GameVoice` — profiles, voices, hotkeys, logs |
| `-KeepVoices` | Leave the ~260 MB of models, for reinstalling later |
| `-Force` | Do not ask |

## Use it

1. Start the game in **borderless windowed** (see [Limits](#limits)).
2. Press **Ctrl+Alt+V**, or click **Start listening**.

That is the whole setup. The default profile reads the bottom third of whatever
window is in front, which is where most games put subtitles.

If nothing is spoken, open **Capture → Test capture now**. It shows the exact
crop, the scale it settled on, and the text it read back.

| Hotkey | |
|---|---|
| `Ctrl+Alt+V` | Start / pause |
| `Ctrl+Alt+X` | Silence |

## How it decides who is speaking

| Source | On screen | Used when |
|---|---|---|
| Name prefix | `Elena: The bridge will not hold.` | Most games |
| Bracketed name | `[Merchant] Finest steel here.` | Subtitle-style captions |
| Name on its own line | `Marcus` above the dialogue | RPGs with a name box |
| A separate name region | the name box read on its own | Set in Capture; most accurate |
| Nothing | plain narration | Falls back to the narrator voice |

Interface text is filtered rather than read: button prompts, page counters,
`Objective:` and `Warning:` labels, and anything matching the profile's ignore
patterns.

## How each character gets a voice

1. **An override you set** in the Voices tab always wins.
2. **Gender is inferred** from titles and roles first (`Lady`, `King`, `Old
   Woman`, `Blacksmith`), then from a bundled list of ~490 given names.
3. **A voice is drawn from that gender's pool** by hashing the character name
   and the game name — so the same character sounds the same on every launch,
   on any machine, with nothing stored.
4. **Pace is nudged** per character, so two characters sharing a voice still
   sound like different people.

An invented fantasy name carries no gender signal, so it draws from the combined
pool. That is a real limit, not a bug — set the gender once in the Voices tab and
it is remembered in the game's profile.

### Where the voices come from

Piper's `en_US-libritts_r-medium` holds **904 speakers in one 78 MB file**, which
is what makes a distinct voice per character practical. It ships no gender
metadata, so `tools/build_voice_table.py` synthesises a probe phrase per speaker
and measures the median fundamental frequency:

| | count | median pitch |
|---|---|---|
| male | 456 | 117 Hz |
| female | 385 | 199 Hz |
| ambiguous (excluded) | 63 | 155–172 Hz |

The classifier was validated against two known-gender voices before the full run
(`ryan-high` → 128.9 Hz male, `hfc_female-medium` → 229.7 Hz female). Speakers in
the undecided band are kept out of the automatic pools rather than guessed at.

## The window

| Tab | |
|---|---|
| **Listening** | Start/pause, live transcript, **Next line**, **Read whole line**, **Silence** |
| **Voices** | Engine, speed, volume, default voices, and a per-character table with gender and voice dropdowns |
| **Capture** | Monitor, drag-a-box region picker, trigger mode, tuning, and **Test capture now** |
| **Profiles** | Create, duplicate, rename, delete, and edit how a game is recognised |

Closing the window keeps it running in the tray. The tray icon is green while
listening, indigo when idle.

## Reading on change, not on a timer

By default GameVoice compares each screen grab with the last and only recognises
text when the picture actually moved:

| | cost per frame |
|---|---|
| Change test | **0.22 ms** |
| Full recognise | **76.7 ms** |

So while dialogue sits still it does almost nothing, and it reads the *instant*
the box changes rather than waiting for the next tick. **On a fixed timer** is
still available for a constantly animating dialogue box.

## Command line

```
gv doctor --delay 5      # switch to the game, then see exactly what it reads
gv say "Hold the gate." --speaker "Sir Roland"
gv voices --gender female
gv download --starter
gv run                   # headless; prints each line as it speaks
gv devices               # OCR languages and audio outputs
```

`gv doctor` is the first thing to reach for when a game is not being read. It
reports the window, the profile chosen, the capture rectangle, the OCR output and
the voice that would be used, so the failing step shows itself.

## Profiles

A profile is one game's settings: capture area, detection tuning, voice choices
and per-character overrides. Shipped profiles live in `profiles/`; anything you
change is saved to `%LOCALAPPDATA%\GameVoice\profiles\` and wins over the shipped
copy, so updating never overwrites a tuned region.

**New** on the Profiles tab fills in the executable and window title from
whatever game is in front. After that, GameVoice switches profiles on its own.

Two templates are included to copy from: `template-name-box` and
`template-visual-novel`.

## Tuning

| Symptom | Fix |
|---|---|
| Nothing is read | **Capture → Test capture now.** A flat-colour preview means exclusive fullscreen — switch to borderless windowed |
| Text stops mid-passage | The capture area is cutting it off. Test reports when text touches an edge; drag a taller region |
| Text is misread | Raise the **upscale limit**, or pick a tighter area around the words |
| Dark text on a light box | Tick **Invert** |
| Lines get cut off | Untick **Cut off the current line when new text appears** (off by default) |
| Interface text is read | Add a regex to the profile's `ignore_patterns` |
| Lines are missed | Lower **Change sensitivity**, or switch to the fixed timer |
| An animated background keeps re-triggering | Raise **Change sensitivity** |

## Limits

Real, and worth knowing before use.

- **Exclusive fullscreen cannot be captured.** Frames come back black.
  Borderless windowed works. GameVoice detects this and says so.
- **A made-up name cannot be gendered.** Set it once in the Voices tab.
- **English only** out of the box. Other languages need a Windows OCR language
  pack plus a matching Piper voice; both are supported, neither is preinstalled.
- **Stylised fonts reduce accuracy.** Decorated fantasy typefaces read worse
  than clean subtitle fonts.
- **It reads what is on screen.** Text that never appears as text is not read.

## Requirements

- Windows 10 1809 or newer / Windows 11
- Python 3.10+
- A Windows OCR language pack (English is present on most installs; add more
  under *Settings → Time & language → Language & region*)
- ~600 MB of disk for the environment and voices

## Architecture

```
capture.py    grab a rectangle; find the game window; detect pixel change
   |
ocr.py        Windows.Media.Ocr, ~4 ms per subtitle band, adaptive upscaling
   |
dialogue.py   decide when text has settled; split off the speaker; chunk long text
   |
voices.py     infer gender; assign a voice deterministically
   |
tts.py        Piper (neural, CPU) or SAPI (built in, zero download)
   |
audio.py      one output stream, grouped clips, skip and interrupt
```

`engine.py` runs a *reader* thread and a *speaker* thread. They are separate so
synthesising a long line never stalls the next screen sample — otherwise dialogue
would be missed while the previous line was still being spoken.

### Load order is a correctness constraint

Importing the Windows Runtime before onnxruntime leaves onnxruntime unable to
initialise its DLL, which disables every Piper voice and reports it as *"Piper is
not installed"*. GameVoice needs both. `native.py` forces the working order,
`ocr.py` calls it before touching WinRT, and `tests/test_native.py` locks it in.

### Measured

| | |
|---|---|
| OCR, cropped subtitle band | ~4 ms |
| Piper synthesis | 80 ms for 3.4 s of speech (RTF 0.025) |
| Change test | 0.22 ms |

## Development

```powershell
.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pytest tests\ -q                      # the full suite
.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pytest tests\ -q -m "not integration" # no OCR or voices needed
.venv\Scripts\python.exe tools\build_voice_table.py               # re-measure after adding voices
.venv\Scripts\python.exe tools\make_icon.py                       # rebuild the icon
```

Integration tests render a synthetic dialogue box and drive the real OCR engine
and real Piper voices, so they need an OCR language pack and at least one voice
model present.

